WEBVTT

00:00.151 --> 00:00.491
So exciting.

00:01.512 --> 00:03.132
Today is February 2, 2026.

00:03.252 --> 00:07.574
My name is Caitlyn Sheets, and I'm currently in the glass clock building, is that right?

00:07.854 --> 00:09.215
In the basement.

00:09.575 --> 00:16.218
I'm here with Lauren, and we're conducting this interview on Texas A&M's campus and College Station.

00:16.538 --> 00:22.401
The interview is part of the oral history project documenting the experiences of Aggies for the 150th celebration of Texas A&M.

00:23.301 --> 00:24.642
Do I consent to report?

00:24.702 --> 00:24.902
Yes.

00:25.222 --> 00:25.522
Awesome.

00:25.542 --> 00:26.242
Thank you so much.

00:26.262 --> 00:29.844
So I'm here with Lauren, as I said, a former undergrad

00:30.382 --> 00:36.586
I was a current grad student as well as an employee.

00:36.606 --> 00:38.067
That's awesome.

00:38.087 --> 00:41.129
Thank you for joining me and sharing your story.

00:41.629 --> 00:42.009
Of course.

00:42.029 --> 00:42.770
I'm so happy to be here.

00:42.790 --> 00:43.210
Thank you.

00:43.650 --> 00:47.132
Can you give me a little bit of background before you came to Texas A&M?

00:47.412 --> 00:47.753
Yeah.

00:48.613 --> 00:52.017
Yeah, so I was born in Cincinnati.

00:52.097 --> 00:54.259
I come from rural Ohio people.

00:54.319 --> 00:57.422
I still say pop, all that good stuff.

00:57.542 --> 01:05.110
There was a scary week there with the college playoffs because A&M would have gone to Ohio State if we beat Miami.

01:05.130 --> 01:08.374
But alas, Carson Beck made that not an issue.

01:08.394 --> 01:09.194
Yeah.

01:09.975 --> 01:13.317
Yeah, so I always imagined I'd go back up to Ohio for school.

01:13.477 --> 01:15.438
All my extended family is still up there.

01:15.458 --> 01:18.179
My dad went to Miami University in Oxford, Ohio.

01:18.219 --> 01:21.321
My mom went to Ohio State for a semester before going to cosmetology school.

01:21.381 --> 01:24.263
So a lot of roots are in Ohio.

01:24.623 --> 01:28.545
We moved down here 20 years ago this summer, which is crazy.

01:29.725 --> 01:31.886
We went to Sugar Land, Missouri City area.

01:31.966 --> 01:34.428
I was there in elementary school until fifth grade.

01:34.488 --> 01:35.929
And then we moved to the Woodlands.

01:36.409 --> 01:38.050
I was there fifth through...

01:38.830 --> 01:39.951
You know, until I went to college.

01:40.671 --> 01:43.033
Yeah, I have two siblings.

01:43.113 --> 01:45.734
One's a twin sister who did not pursue higher ed at all.

01:46.155 --> 01:49.797
And then my brother who went to A&M who kind of fed me the Kool-Aid.

01:51.178 --> 01:54.420
Yes, my sister and I visited him one weekend and I was like,

01:54.560 --> 01:55.461
This isn't so bad.

01:55.561 --> 01:56.202
Yeah, sure.

01:56.222 --> 02:04.390
And then after high school, I was encouraged slash you're going to do Lone Star Community College for a year.

02:04.410 --> 02:11.378
I only had to do it a year instead of two to get my full associate since I graduated high school with like 27 college credits.

02:11.398 --> 02:16.903
Yeah, my high school is a very, very huge proponent for AP, dual credits, the Woodlands High School.

02:17.624 --> 02:17.864
Yes.

02:18.365 --> 02:19.325
Yeah, we're a 6A.

02:19.505 --> 02:24.048
It's, it's hard to explain, but everything's competition and the woodlands.

02:24.068 --> 02:25.529
Like if you, yes, it is.

02:25.589 --> 02:29.491
If you want to do varsity or be the lead in the, I did theater in high school.

02:29.631 --> 02:34.254
So if you wanted to do leads, I was on the improv troupe in high school.

02:34.454 --> 02:39.117
I was the president for two years and it was pretty much, you have to start doing that when you're five for a chance.

02:39.197 --> 02:40.138
Cause it's a 6A.

02:40.218 --> 02:41.259
I graduated with 1100 kids.

02:43.039 --> 02:44.820
So it's a different culture.

02:46.061 --> 02:49.484
Yes, it's very competitive, academics and athletics equally.

02:49.944 --> 02:53.327
I think that's kind of hard to find, but again, it's Texas, so yeah.

02:55.973 --> 03:25.380
present in your mind yes I wouldn't say though I tried in high school English and English particularly became very very easy to me I was always a really big reader as a kid I have a edition of Little House on the Prairie on my bookshelf um that is a good example of why I am the way I am today the the breadcrumbs were always always there um I just I originally was actually going to study English um but they recommended to transfer into A&M you already have your foreign language done and I did not

03:26.200 --> 03:30.643
And so the BS of history here at A&M offers without the foreign language.

03:30.983 --> 03:33.064
And that was the only other major I was interested in.

03:33.084 --> 03:34.965
So I kind of naturally went in there.

03:34.985 --> 03:37.507
I want to say that might have been the first year they offered that.

03:37.587 --> 03:39.828
So the timing was very, very kismet.

03:39.848 --> 03:40.889
And I'm very, very thankful.

03:41.929 --> 03:42.870
And I was an English minor.

03:43.010 --> 03:44.531
So it's still a big part of my life.

03:44.571 --> 03:47.312
It's a big philosophy of my history training and all that.

03:47.372 --> 03:48.313
But yeah.

03:48.493 --> 03:48.613
Yeah.

03:48.933 --> 03:50.355
Academics have always been a big part of me.

03:50.395 --> 03:52.918
It's definitely been, I was embarrassed to get B's.

03:53.659 --> 03:56.563
I was that kid in English who, like, if Lauren didn't read, we're screwed.

03:56.944 --> 03:58.205
Like, I'd always be that person.

03:58.746 --> 04:00.809
Yeah, math and science are always pretty hard for me.

04:00.889 --> 04:04.233
I never really... Yeah, I never really got that, but...

04:06.108 --> 04:33.607
teacher professor that really encouraged you yeah I had a wonderful junior and senior so both of those were dual credits um Dr. Heidi Bardenhagen was my junior and then um Dr. no he's not a doctor sorry uh Jeff Murren was my senior and like he used to be the only teacher who taught senior year dual credits so it was like my brother had him when he taught at McCullough the junior high school so then he moved to the high school he's just amazing he's tatted up and he's just awesome and

04:34.267 --> 04:35.067
I love that man.

04:35.167 --> 04:36.448
He's just wonderful, wonderful.

04:36.748 --> 04:40.789
And Dr. Barton Hagen, obviously overqualified.

04:44.930 --> 04:48.171
Just tried everything to make sure we knew the more analytical side of English.

04:48.191 --> 04:51.132
And it's not just the curtains were blue, which symbolizes sadness.

04:51.372 --> 04:51.932
Like it was.

04:52.592 --> 04:54.113
Yeah, but she was unfortunately

04:55.950 --> 04:57.211
ran out of the woodlands.

04:57.731 --> 05:02.214
She was teaching lenses of English, and one of them is a Marxist lens.

05:02.294 --> 05:06.736
She was not saying she's a huge Marxist, but there was a kid who was part of the Turning Point USA club.

05:07.237 --> 05:12.099
He brought that to parents, and she was alleviated of her duties.

05:12.119 --> 05:12.500
That's wild.

05:13.340 --> 05:13.900
Yeah, absolutely.

05:13.920 --> 05:15.161
But that's still happening.

05:15.261 --> 05:15.681
Absolutely.

05:15.721 --> 05:16.742
That was 2023?

05:16.802 --> 05:17.022
2023.

05:17.082 --> 05:20.664
2023 would still be the Marxist is scary.

05:20.684 --> 05:21.725
The thing is...

05:26.153 --> 05:31.998
It's the most frustrating because she left academia because she said it focused too much on research and she just wanted to teach.

05:33.119 --> 05:34.200
That's so rare.

05:34.260 --> 05:34.821
That's so rare.

05:35.021 --> 05:35.521
No, I know.

05:35.861 --> 05:40.626
And she could have gone to a private or a small liberal arts school, but she chose dual credit English.

05:41.446 --> 05:41.646
Yeah.

05:42.387 --> 05:43.608
Yeah.

05:43.708 --> 05:53.397
I really credit them too for, you know, like if I do defend successfully my PhD, like I would definitely make sure to knock on wood to find them and be like, you are a huge part of this.

05:53.597 --> 05:53.777
So.

05:55.518 --> 06:12.847
seeing where it comes from oh yeah no totally and like so i particularly focus on native american history and i was torn between that or holocaust studies two very uh light-hearted areas of history um but growing up my parents favorite movie one of theirs is dances with wolves

06:13.727 --> 06:26.574
And obviously it's not historically the best, but it was just always kind of in the background or my parents are really big history fans, maybe not in the academic sense, but more of just in popular culture or a documentary.

06:27.274 --> 06:34.498
My mom's a huge reader, so it's always been encouraged to, you know, listen to the real quote unquote real story or

06:35.078 --> 06:35.879
Give space for that.

06:35.999 --> 06:36.219
Yeah.

06:36.379 --> 06:36.659
Absolutely.

06:36.679 --> 06:38.461
Can I ask if your parents are educated?

06:39.442 --> 06:40.402
Yeah.

06:40.442 --> 06:42.184
My father went to Miami University.

06:42.444 --> 06:44.666
It is in Oxford, Ohio.

06:44.866 --> 06:45.547
I almost went there.

06:45.567 --> 06:47.248
I was accepted, but it was $30,000 for out-of-state.

06:50.063 --> 06:51.623
Not really worth that.

06:53.064 --> 06:59.185
But yeah, my dad, he majored in sports management, but now he is the VP of an automobile company for supply chain.

06:59.385 --> 07:03.705
So just because you get a degree in something doesn't mean that's what you have to do.

07:04.646 --> 07:08.586
And then my mom went to Ohio State for a semester before she went to cosmetology school.

07:08.766 --> 07:13.307
So me being A&M, graduating, very, very big deal.

07:13.447 --> 07:17.328
My mom does not take this for granted and really instilled that in me and Rachel, my twin sister,

07:18.260 --> 07:39.153
try to do something because yeah that's yeah no that's the one thing that you really can't get taken away from you is that education and the skills you learn in the classroom that's very true um so come from a very academic family or even even seems like a hard looking yes yes um so did you ever feel a lot of pressure or have those

07:41.075 --> 07:44.996
No, I never felt like if I didn't bring home an A, I'd be grounded for a month.

07:45.076 --> 07:48.298
It was more of just don't be a dumbass.

07:48.998 --> 07:51.038
It's kind of expected of just you excel.

07:51.339 --> 07:54.500
You know, I was never pressured to have an actual job in high school.

07:54.920 --> 07:55.900
School was the job.

07:57.601 --> 08:00.102
So it was just try to never to quit at anything.

08:00.142 --> 08:00.882
You make a commitment.

08:00.902 --> 08:01.722
You got to do it.

08:01.782 --> 08:03.803
That was as well as theater and improv.

08:04.743 --> 08:06.664
Just give 110% to everything you do.

08:07.924 --> 08:09.805
My mom's dad farmed for 40 years, so...

08:11.153 --> 08:11.353
Yeah.

08:12.034 --> 08:12.595
Hardworking.

08:12.615 --> 08:13.156
Yeah.

08:13.196 --> 08:13.416
Yeah.

08:14.057 --> 08:16.721
Do you think that those traits have followed you through?

08:17.002 --> 08:17.142
Mm-hmm.

08:23.607 --> 08:25.968
I think just not taking it for granted.

08:25.988 --> 08:32.610
I think sometimes you see kids on campus who college to them was never a question.

08:33.911 --> 08:47.235
It's just something they have to do to check off the box and maybe they're not appreciating of like, this might be the one time in my life where I just get to read something and talk about it and try to expand yourself as a human, as a scholar.

08:47.255 --> 08:49.496
I think that's so, so, so special.

08:49.976 --> 08:52.197
And I think it's maybe the first time

08:53.050 --> 09:01.035
in a lot of society's history where a majority of its populace are educated, literate, and trying to improve in that area.

09:01.295 --> 09:02.956
Very good point.

09:04.297 --> 09:08.120
So we talked a little bit about this, about where history came from.

09:09.490 --> 09:11.951
What made you choose Native American history?

09:12.192 --> 09:13.112
Yeah, I can tell you exactly.

09:13.172 --> 09:18.475
So I was taking Native American history, a little survey course with Dr. Angela Hudson.

09:19.396 --> 09:23.338
I was in M207, this building over here on campus.

09:23.658 --> 09:24.759
It's the A in engineering.

09:25.079 --> 09:26.920
For some reason, a lot of history seminars are in there.

09:26.940 --> 09:30.622
It's like a huge 400-person seminar room.

09:31.342 --> 09:35.905
And I just remember sitting there one day, and I'm like, this is what I want to do.

09:36.960 --> 10:03.062
um obviously it's not a i'm an outsider i am not indigenous culturally um related i'm not enrolled in any federally recognized tribes or state recognized tribes um but it makes you view america in maybe a different light a more more honest more truthful it's a harder light to look at it but it makes you feel more connected to the land its people its cultures um

10:04.303 --> 10:10.148
Yeah, and it's, you know, not typically the first thing or maybe in bold when you read textbooks.

10:10.208 --> 10:14.031
Maybe it's a little side and then once we get to the Trail of Tears, it's never really brought up again.

10:14.092 --> 10:18.355
And that's something I'm very passionate about is bringing it into the main historical conversation.

10:20.497 --> 10:22.639
Yeah, I mean, yeah.

10:22.679 --> 10:24.460
And Dr. Hudson is now serving as my advisor.

10:24.921 --> 10:26.602
So it's full circle.

10:26.742 --> 10:30.866
It was just a once in a lifetime opportunity.

10:30.906 --> 10:31.606
Really, really, really.

10:32.006 --> 10:32.207
Yeah.

10:33.105 --> 10:35.006
Um, was the first time you met her was in that class?

10:35.507 --> 10:35.707
Yes.

10:36.267 --> 10:36.467
Yes.

10:36.607 --> 10:38.028
But it's pretty, it's a bigger class.

10:38.108 --> 10:40.570
So I don't know if we ever got a lot on one-on-one time.

10:41.070 --> 10:45.213
And then, um, that following semester, so fall, no, spring of 23.

10:45.273 --> 10:46.094
Yeah.

10:46.434 --> 10:47.074
Spring of 23.

10:47.715 --> 10:53.178
Um, she was teaching her smaller 300 level class called Native Americans of the South.

10:54.279 --> 10:55.100
And I took that as well.

10:56.240 --> 10:58.382
And again, just completely fall in love with it.

10:58.522 --> 11:00.383
I would consider myself a Southern historian.

11:01.164 --> 11:01.324
Um,

11:02.084 --> 11:03.987
And I actually met a future roommate there.

11:04.007 --> 11:08.433
Yeah, like, yeah, like just has overfull in my cup in so many ways.

11:08.513 --> 11:12.698
But that's when we definitely had a way closer intimate working relationship.

11:13.239 --> 11:16.704
And then that summer, I was graduating early in August.

11:16.724 --> 11:18.386
So I'm class of 25, but graduated.

11:19.988 --> 11:21.489
May, no, August to 24.

11:21.909 --> 11:22.990
So I'm like a full year early.

11:23.671 --> 11:27.655
And I basically reached out to her and said like, hey, I need a job.

11:27.775 --> 11:29.637
Like, do you need someone to refill coffee?

11:29.737 --> 11:31.719
Like anything, anything, anything I will do for you.

11:32.199 --> 11:34.201
And she said like, I was actually about to reach out to you.

11:34.801 --> 11:36.703
I need a research assistant for a project.

11:37.524 --> 11:40.126
And that's what kind of got me on board to do actual...

11:40.967 --> 11:43.208
factual history and not just reading about it.

11:43.228 --> 11:48.150
I was researching a group of Native Americans who were here between 1904 to 1911.

11:48.830 --> 11:51.631
They were football players here before we officially integrated.

11:52.391 --> 11:54.292
And it's transnational.

11:54.312 --> 11:55.813
We're dealing with people from Oklahoma.

11:56.853 --> 11:58.194
We have a Cheyenne individual.

11:58.294 --> 12:01.155
They're from Carlisle, the boarding school in Carlisle, Pennsylvania.

12:02.555 --> 12:10.999
Again, just completely transforms what you think of A&M when we realize who was actually here taking classes before maybe we were in that national.

12:11.399 --> 12:12.000
Right, yeah.

12:13.583 --> 12:14.644
Attention spotlight?

12:14.744 --> 12:15.744
Yeah.

12:16.044 --> 12:18.365
So we worked on that research together for a year.

12:18.445 --> 12:22.848
We presented a paper together at the Alliance for Texas History conference in May of 25.

12:22.868 --> 12:26.029
That was my first conference.

12:26.229 --> 12:31.192
And then I basically told her, like, hey, I'm applying for a PhD.

12:31.232 --> 12:33.233
Like, I really want to do this for the rest of my life.

12:34.549 --> 12:36.090
fills me, like this is amazing.

12:37.051 --> 12:37.912
So yeah, here we are.

12:38.292 --> 12:43.536
I am finished with my first semester as of fall of 25, and now we're entering spring 26.

12:43.556 --> 12:44.057
Congratulations.

12:44.777 --> 12:45.878
Yes, well thank you.

12:45.898 --> 12:51.363
Actually, can you go back and talk a little bit about the Native Americans that were here before?

12:51.383 --> 12:51.483
Yeah.

12:51.603 --> 12:53.245
Because I had no knowledge.

12:53.265 --> 12:53.705
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

12:54.245 --> 12:57.148
So we found six in this time frame.

12:57.588 --> 13:02.172
All of them but one are Choctaw, which we think there might be something there.

13:03.920 --> 13:07.802
And the other one I mentioned, Cheyenne, his name is Michael Valenti.

13:07.862 --> 13:10.743
He is actually related from someone who survived wounded knee.

13:11.583 --> 13:13.705
His mother survived wounded knee.

13:14.205 --> 13:18.147
And he actually went on to marry, she's Alaskan native.

13:18.807 --> 13:23.249
Her name is Cecilia Baranovich, and her dad was actually a Russian trapper mogul.

13:23.690 --> 13:24.030
So...

13:25.030 --> 13:25.971
Insane.

13:25.991 --> 13:30.152
And Michael went on after being here at A&M.

13:30.573 --> 13:32.113
He coached for Baylor.

13:32.754 --> 13:37.756
He had an amazing career in the minor league baseball as well as professionals.

13:37.796 --> 13:40.157
He was part of the Cincinnati Reds for a little bit, so that was...

13:40.717 --> 13:42.458
As a Cincinnati native, that was fun.

13:43.258 --> 13:45.859
And then we also have George Marlowe.

13:47.119 --> 13:48.460
He was also Choctaw.

13:48.840 --> 13:59.184
Oh gosh, we have Sig Flincham, James Flincham, nicknamed Sig, who we think is married during his time here, which is odd because where was his wife staying?

14:00.164 --> 14:08.047
So unfortunately, a lot more questions are not answered with these men because there's a lot of questions about if they're being paid or not.

14:08.067 --> 14:08.127
Oh.

14:10.780 --> 14:38.213
because we have records of michael balenti and um victor kelly called by chalk as he's choctaw chalk kelly being paid at carlisle so it's kind of hard to imagine why they would leave their income to texas when they have absolutely no ties right here um what years is this i'm sorry yeah no you're right it's between about 1904 to 1911 okay yes and this is a football period in which we have the head coach of um

14:38.958 --> 14:40.940
Charles Moran, Uncle Charlie.

14:42.562 --> 14:43.383
Yes.

14:43.403 --> 14:45.846
So this is the only other time where A&M stopped playing UT.

14:47.508 --> 14:54.717
There was claims that he had professionals, which might have been those Carlisle native players, if they're being paid.

14:55.358 --> 14:58.882
And basically they said, we're not playing you until Moran's gone.

14:59.842 --> 15:01.023
And we didn't play them for three years.

15:01.043 --> 15:03.125
That's wild.

15:03.805 --> 15:07.188
So, yeah, we have a lot of evidence of them being involved in clubs.

15:07.708 --> 15:13.172
They were very active in Ross Volunteers, which is ironic, as Sol Ross is a proclaimed, quote-unquote, Indian killer.

15:13.473 --> 15:20.078
