17:35.939 --> 17:36.399
So many.

17:36.760 --> 17:37.240
So many.

17:37.300 --> 17:40.282
We have so many ags from India.

17:41.863 --> 17:43.925
I mean, it's crazy, crazy, crazy.

17:44.005 --> 17:52.751
And they're proudly in these yearbooks because the most interesting thing about my native ags, which I forgot to mention, none of them graduated until Bill Leftwich in 1954.

17:54.852 --> 17:57.955
So they're here for four years plus.

17:58.515 --> 17:59.756
None of them have officially graduated.

18:02.187 --> 18:03.928
We think that might have been they're only taking a class or two.

18:05.268 --> 18:08.570
But then this is at a time when the whole school is working on the core schedule.

18:09.390 --> 18:10.711
So what are you doing all day?

18:11.711 --> 18:12.732
What are you doing all day?

18:14.090 --> 18:14.951
Very strict.

18:15.571 --> 18:15.911
Yeah.

18:15.931 --> 18:21.255
I mean, there's in the yearbook of like what time breakfast is, what time march is, what time is everyone walking the fourth period?

18:21.275 --> 18:24.597
Like it's a very military school.

18:24.677 --> 18:25.618
It 100 percent is.

18:25.658 --> 18:28.560
So we just want to know, did that cause animosity?

18:28.620 --> 18:33.684
Well, clearly not, because Victor Kelly was awarded most popular cadet for superlatives in one of the yearbooks.

18:35.485 --> 18:37.948
So these are very well-known people on campus.

18:38.789 --> 18:41.872
So it is fascinating that there is no sign of them today.

18:42.373 --> 18:48.539
When arguably the beginnings of A&M football being commercially successful is this team.

18:53.351 --> 18:53.891
Yes, it is.

18:54.312 --> 18:55.532
Because I'm a football nut.

18:55.632 --> 18:56.353
I really am.

18:56.673 --> 18:59.914
I obviously the amount of money the school's invested into it.

19:00.354 --> 19:12.280
I think that's partly why of like, okay, well, if there's some areas of the school that are not as funded, but we're putting hundreds of million dollars into our coaching salaries and NIL and our facilities.

19:13.723 --> 19:15.023
I'm going to check in.

19:16.784 --> 19:28.708
Obviously, there's a lot of literature on native sport, obviously with Jim Thorpe and, you know, Carlisle football team completely transformed the game as we know it.

19:28.948 --> 19:32.289
So they're very, very interconnected and related.

19:32.529 --> 19:33.709
So it's very exciting.

19:33.909 --> 19:36.030
Another side thing.

19:36.590 --> 19:41.992
I think we just, not inherited, but we're now housing all of the old films.

19:42.576 --> 19:42.936
Reels?

19:43.216 --> 19:43.556
Mm-hmm.

19:44.356 --> 19:45.377
From the football?

19:45.397 --> 19:45.557
Mm-hmm.

19:46.017 --> 19:46.497
Do you?

19:46.577 --> 19:47.017
Mm-hmm.

19:47.037 --> 19:47.657
Yep, okay.

19:48.157 --> 19:49.958
Mm-hmm.

19:50.538 --> 19:51.918
I'm going to cry.

19:52.018 --> 19:52.118
Oh!

19:52.138 --> 19:52.898
Because there's photos.

19:52.918 --> 19:56.519
We have photos of them playing these games sometimes, and it's like just being able to pick them out.

19:56.539 --> 19:56.739
Right.

19:57.439 --> 20:03.020
Because only Victor Kelly, I'd say, is phenotypically someone you could appoint as indigenous.

20:03.720 --> 20:04.001
Mm-hmm.

20:04.521 --> 20:05.361
So there's something to that.

20:05.861 --> 20:06.061
Very much.

20:06.381 --> 20:09.103
And they're calling him Choctaw.

20:09.624 --> 20:11.645
They are not hiding their nativeness.

20:12.366 --> 20:16.189
In the yearbook, they hail, oh, he's from the new state of Oklahoma.

20:16.749 --> 20:17.890
He does the corn dance.

20:18.090 --> 20:19.091
He does the war whoops.

20:19.592 --> 20:20.292
But it's fascinating.

20:20.352 --> 20:24.055
The more they become integrated into campus, the less they comment on their nativeness.

20:25.196 --> 20:26.817
That's interesting.

20:26.837 --> 20:33.583
So we kind of question, is this Aggie, the binding force, is this able to trump racial lines?

20:36.175 --> 20:36.776
There's something to that.

20:36.796 --> 20:37.396
That's strong.

20:37.456 --> 20:38.537
Yeah, there's something to that.

20:38.597 --> 20:39.118
There truly is.

20:39.138 --> 20:39.338
Yeah.

20:40.940 --> 20:41.120
Yeah.

20:42.361 --> 20:45.824
This is also going to be a random side thing, but you brought up the Aggie spirit.

20:45.844 --> 20:46.124
Mm-hmm.

20:46.845 --> 20:50.869
And I've seen, or I've heard so many different people say what it means to them.

20:50.929 --> 20:51.169
Mm-hmm.

20:51.209 --> 20:52.631
What does that mean to you?

20:55.433 --> 21:00.298
I would say really and truly just taking the harder path, if it's the right path.

21:01.912 --> 21:03.994
I would say that is probably the Aggie way.

21:04.194 --> 21:06.756
Like, why am I getting choked up?

21:07.276 --> 21:08.657
My parents were at the airport the other day.

21:08.737 --> 21:11.179
They didn't go to A&M, but they were wearing A&M merch.

21:11.780 --> 21:15.302
And they saw a young mom, just alone, just kind of struggling.

21:15.362 --> 21:18.785
We had a toddler crying and, like, another kind of pulling on her arm.

21:18.825 --> 21:20.226
And she's obviously having a hard time.

21:20.266 --> 21:22.568
And my mom just walks up to her and says, like, can I hold?

21:22.588 --> 21:24.009
Do you need to go to the bathroom or something?

21:24.029 --> 21:24.930
And we can watch them.

21:25.430 --> 21:27.332
And she said, like, well, y'all are Aggies.

21:28.032 --> 21:28.613
Of course.

21:28.633 --> 21:28.993
Mm-hmm.

21:29.786 --> 21:37.289
So, you know, and I know people make fun of it, of cult or whatever, but you can go anywhere, anywhere, anywhere, and you will find someone.

21:37.630 --> 21:43.152
And that is just enough to share a meal or lend a helping hand.

21:43.192 --> 21:45.113
And I think that is so, so, so important today.

21:45.313 --> 21:46.114
That's so powerful.

21:46.494 --> 21:46.894
Truly.

21:46.914 --> 21:47.094
Yeah.

21:48.575 --> 21:51.496
And I hear all these stories about people that they're giving their senior ring.

21:51.636 --> 21:51.996
Oh, yeah.

21:52.076 --> 21:57.359
And then they see somebody else in the wild with it, and they just instantly have that connection.

21:57.379 --> 21:57.899
Yeah, yeah.

22:02.072 --> 22:03.012
all is very new.

22:03.032 --> 22:03.633
Oh, absolutely.

22:04.893 --> 22:07.034
Yeah, it is a whole new world.

22:07.734 --> 22:16.298
And part of it breaks my heart because I want to say out of the 12 of my cohort for grad school, maybe only two of us went to undergrad here.

22:18.479 --> 22:20.320
So they don't love it like we do.

22:20.740 --> 22:22.200
It doesn't maybe mean as much to them.

22:23.961 --> 22:30.444
You know, they came here specifically for a professor or maybe the stipend was the most handsome or something like that.

22:31.872 --> 22:36.873
So they didn't get to really experience all the charm that A&M has to offer.

22:38.573 --> 22:43.714
So, yeah, I do try to, you know, amp it up or say, like, you really should go to a football game.

22:43.754 --> 22:44.394
Like, you're here.

22:44.814 --> 22:45.815
Baseball is super fun.

22:45.835 --> 22:47.975
The whole town doesn't shut down for baseball.

22:48.015 --> 22:49.215
It's easier to get in and out.

22:49.275 --> 22:53.196
And, you know, our women volleyball team just brought home our first national title.

22:53.296 --> 22:54.776
And it's just very exciting.

22:54.796 --> 22:55.836
Basketball is doing good.

22:55.856 --> 22:57.637
I had no idea what that would look like.

22:57.657 --> 22:58.477
Yeah.

22:59.037 --> 23:02.198
Yeah, I mean, it's just a very, very exciting time.

23:04.219 --> 23:04.639
Athletic.

23:05.279 --> 23:06.379
Athletically, yes.

23:07.020 --> 23:07.740
Yes, athletically.

23:09.500 --> 23:17.203
So obviously you were interested in history as an undergraduate, but what made you continue on as that?

23:17.223 --> 23:17.563
Dr. Hudson.

23:19.096 --> 23:19.897
Yes, Dr. Hudson.

23:21.498 --> 23:26.061
Native Studies is kind of a smaller area.

23:27.161 --> 23:30.704
My husband's also getting his master's at A&M in engineering.

23:30.804 --> 23:34.186
I joke A&M owns us, but we just bought a house here.

23:34.206 --> 23:35.907
We're putting roots down.

23:36.588 --> 23:38.689
We're guaranteed here for five years with my program.

23:38.969 --> 23:40.791
So it just makes the most sense.

23:41.031 --> 23:44.293
He also works full-time, so I did not apply to anywhere else.

23:44.933 --> 23:45.194
This is it?

23:45.494 --> 23:48.456
Yes, and I only applied out of Lone Star Community College to A&M.

23:50.725 --> 23:51.265
That's impressive.

23:51.926 --> 23:52.686
People say don't do that.

23:53.227 --> 23:57.911
I've got a lot of people say do not do that for undergrad and graduate, but I knew.

23:57.931 --> 23:59.172
If you love it, you love it.

23:59.192 --> 23:59.432
I love it.

23:59.592 --> 24:04.396
So, yeah, I'll be an ag for, oh gosh, undergrad was three.

24:05.417 --> 24:06.097
Yeah, almost eight years.

24:06.458 --> 24:07.238
Almost a decade.

24:07.579 --> 24:08.740
Yeah.

24:08.780 --> 24:08.900
Yeah.

24:09.120 --> 24:11.542
You're almost an old ag now.

24:11.942 --> 24:13.203
Almost old ag, yeah.

24:14.564 --> 24:15.005
Almost old ag, yeah.

24:15.025 --> 24:19.028
It's funny to me that the agies that I've talked to, when they...

24:19.643 --> 24:22.905
they didn't go here, but they came as a professor or something.

24:22.925 --> 24:27.428
And they say, they don't, they feel like they can't claim being an Aggie.

24:27.448 --> 24:28.828
And I'm like, you taught here for 25 years.

24:28.969 --> 24:29.629
That makes me sad.

24:29.749 --> 24:33.071
It makes me sad because you're shaping these Aggies.

24:34.012 --> 24:42.176
They're learning how, what that Aggie spirit work ethic looks like through you and the rigor that you are demanding from them.

24:42.317 --> 24:43.917
That's 110% part of it.

24:44.818 --> 24:46.159
Yeah, that upsets me.

24:49.132 --> 25:11.613
makes me really sad yeah i hear no i mean yeah i didn't go to miami or ohio state but you know you feel that connection through family or through just being associated with it for time so i think i have no problem if you teach your room right yeah or like even i encourage grad kids to get their ag hearing why wouldn't you

25:13.076 --> 25:14.458
I know it's expensive.

25:15.099 --> 25:18.865
You put in the work and it's a wonderful tradition that not every school honors.

25:18.885 --> 25:22.150
I mean, we have one of the highest graduation rates in Texas.

25:22.570 --> 25:23.091
Absolutely.

25:23.712 --> 25:24.553
Yes, absolutely.

25:24.773 --> 25:26.456
I want to say we're in the nineties now.

25:27.358 --> 25:28.138
If not 90s, 80s.

25:28.158 --> 25:29.419
And that's unheard of.

25:29.439 --> 25:30.119
That's unheard of.

25:30.439 --> 25:30.960
Absolutely.

25:30.980 --> 25:34.181
Like, I don't think I've literally heard of universities like that.

25:34.721 --> 25:37.122
No, because it is, it's a part.

25:37.303 --> 25:41.464
If you say to someone, I went to A&M and you don't see their ring, you ask, well, is it getting resized?

25:41.845 --> 25:42.365
What's wrong?

25:42.425 --> 25:43.605
Is something wrong?

25:43.745 --> 25:44.346
It's a part of it.

25:44.366 --> 25:45.646
It's 110% part of it.

25:45.666 --> 25:46.887
I mean, I have a paperweight.

25:48.027 --> 25:58.651
Like when Will, my husband got his Agering, my brother got his Agering, and me, my mom, there's this Etsy seller that makes like a wooden ornament of it with your year.

25:59.951 --> 26:01.211
Yeah, like 110%.

26:01.291 --> 26:01.731
Yeah.

26:04.012 --> 26:06.753
So your brother, what was his deal?

26:06.773 --> 26:07.333
Parks and Rec.

26:08.354 --> 26:08.574
Yeah.

26:08.894 --> 26:09.554
Awesome.

26:10.454 --> 26:14.896
Yes, we put the and in A and M. We are not A or M, but yes.

26:15.356 --> 26:15.576
Yeah.

26:17.198 --> 26:18.543
Mm-hmm.

26:18.583 --> 26:19.706
Yeah.

26:19.726 --> 26:21.151
Does he work at an actual park?

26:21.720 --> 26:24.941
So he actually just accepted a new job.

26:24.961 --> 26:25.882
We're very, very excited for him.

26:25.982 --> 26:27.462
He is an aquatics man.

26:27.963 --> 26:33.085
So he runs and pretty much makes sure that lifeguards are being trained properly.

26:33.125 --> 26:33.885
Equipment is good.

26:33.925 --> 26:34.845
Patrons are happy.

26:34.905 --> 26:35.826
Pools are clean.

26:36.306 --> 26:37.326
It's running smoothly.

26:37.346 --> 26:38.047
That's what he does.

26:38.367 --> 26:41.668
He did that for the Woodlands Township for 10 years.

26:41.688 --> 26:43.969
He was there from 16 to 25.

26:44.609 --> 26:46.310
And now he just moved to San Antonio for that.

26:46.350 --> 26:49.851
So he's doing 23 facilities, one year-round natatorium.

26:50.012 --> 26:51.012
We could not be more excited for him.

26:51.172 --> 26:51.653
That's awesome.

26:51.673 --> 26:51.833
Yeah.

26:52.253 --> 26:55.377
That's amazing.

26:57.500 --> 27:01.885
So Dr. Hudson, what, okay, she also is Native American studies as well, right?

27:02.125 --> 27:02.345
Yes.

27:02.626 --> 27:02.806
Okay.

27:03.500 --> 27:04.501
So what is her teaching style?

27:04.621 --> 27:07.023
Because I've never taken a quiz here, obviously.

27:07.584 --> 27:07.784
Yeah.

27:08.325 --> 27:22.518
So I would definitely say she is trying to get you prepared for asking questions that you just have to approach every single text with, every single primary document with, in every single archive trip.

27:22.958 --> 27:23.919
What am I asking?

27:23.939 --> 27:24.459
Who wrote it?

27:24.479 --> 27:25.220
Where do they go to school?

27:25.240 --> 27:25.961
Who's their advisor?

27:27.786 --> 27:28.546
It's all connected.

27:28.666 --> 27:31.527
She really stresses that.

27:31.567 --> 27:32.928
What sources are they engaging in?

27:33.448 --> 27:34.508
What secondary sources?

27:34.568 --> 27:35.829
Who's she pushing back on?

27:35.909 --> 27:41.211
Whose work are they building upon or who are they kind of disputing?

27:43.392 --> 27:50.454
When you go into an archive, it can be simple as take a picture of what you want and then write a little note to yourself because you're going to forget in two weeks why you took a photo of that.

27:50.854 --> 27:51.615
She taught me...

27:53.082 --> 27:55.504
try to be kind to your future forgetful self.

27:56.265 --> 28:00.308
She says that sometimes and I always try to remember that.

28:01.188 --> 28:02.369
She's very primary based.

28:03.931 --> 28:10.756
Oh yeah, in our 300 level class, she'd have a worksheet of like, okay, pull out a line, explain the source they got this from.

28:10.896 --> 28:13.098
Is this a good interpretation of the source?

28:14.051 --> 28:17.732
You don't see that a lot in history classes at like those levels.

28:17.792 --> 28:29.157
It's mainly, again, I think it's just a misconception of the history field in general of just regurgitating a timeline or something that we completely diverge from past 100 level class, especially here at A&M.

28:30.597 --> 28:36.940
But yeah, she's a wonderful, wonderful person to learn how to dissect, look at that document in the context.

28:37.000 --> 28:38.060
How does that impact?

28:38.520 --> 28:40.741
She also comes with an English background.

28:41.361 --> 28:42.862
She has a master's in English as well.

28:43.998 --> 28:47.840
Um, so reading, I think reading.

28:47.860 --> 28:48.580
Yeah.

28:48.620 --> 28:53.141
And that's what a lot of my personal statement was for the grad program of like, I have a huge reading background.

28:53.222 --> 28:54.182
I am a huge reader.

28:54.762 --> 28:58.824
And I read to work in over at the English department was incredibly impactful to me.

28:59.524 --> 29:02.625
Um, just meeting the source where it's at.

29:03.085 --> 29:03.345
Right.

29:03.445 --> 29:03.926
Yeah.

29:03.986 --> 29:04.366
110%.

29:04.426 --> 29:07.827
Um, when you were undergrad, you said you were English, correct?

29:08.748 --> 29:28.021
um what professors there like what kind of skills were transferable to yeah so i was an english minor so i took um you know the comp and rec at the dual credit level in high school so then when i got here i took um oh gosh

29:30.572 --> 29:37.037
A 19th century American novel with Ira DeWorkin, who's fabulous, who wrote a rec letter for me.

29:37.218 --> 29:41.501
I took Chaucer with Dr. Britt Mize, all in Middle English.

29:41.681 --> 29:43.363
I read The Canterbury Tales, all in Middle English.

29:43.503 --> 29:44.363
That was fantastic.

29:45.224 --> 29:48.226
And then with Dr. Cooper, I took...

29:50.348 --> 29:51.771
American novel, World War II and on.

29:52.813 --> 30:02.710
And as far as Dr. Orkin goes, again, just it's really easy to bring the 21st century with you when you're looking at stuff, when you're reading Uncle Tom's Cabin.

30:03.603 --> 30:28.541
when you're reading um you know squatter in the dawn or something like that um when you're reading mark twain lord you want to you want to make it accessible to you but you just can't do that all the time and he would assign a healthy portion every night you'd come to class reading 100 pages each time and that does prep you for grad school but also just how can i read efficiently and effectively um and then brett mys

30:29.930 --> 30:39.513
He was wonderful, and the very first day of class, it's a 400-level Chaucer class, and he said something in Middle English, basically saying, this is how it would sound.

30:40.013 --> 30:42.674
And I swear to you, I heard two kids drop right then and there.

30:42.814 --> 30:44.735
Like, I cannot do this.

30:46.655 --> 30:47.976
Oh, my gosh.

30:48.716 --> 30:49.916
Really, really stuck with me.

30:50.076 --> 30:51.237
Just aspects of Chaucer.

30:52.497 --> 30:55.098
You know, Canterbury Towels, it's literally...

30:55.938 --> 31:24.400
a tale telling contest on a very serious pilgrimage so it's just between it's a balance between um earnest and game and that is where i really rest how i approach a grad seminar when i'm answering questions because i think academia is very very stuffy and unacceptable and it is this wall and it doesn't have to be so i try to always manage the earnestness and game and whatever i'm doing um

31:25.507 --> 31:31.288
And then that last English class I took, there's a wonderful book called Sex and the Cherry by Jeanette Winterson.

31:31.348 --> 31:34.128
It's a postmodernist work and it's just absurd and it's awesome.

31:34.268 --> 31:35.129
And I highly recommend.

31:35.889 --> 31:36.589
It's just good.

31:36.709 --> 31:37.489
It's just good stuff.

31:37.609 --> 31:45.010
And, you know, there's no one universal truth, all that good stuff post nihilism that you get from World War II.

31:45.370 --> 31:53.772
So having you actually, on one hand, people say history is very one way linear.

31:53.932 --> 31:54.832
And then on the other hand,

31:55.976 --> 31:56.416
There is no one truth.

31:56.436 --> 31:56.516
Yes.

31:56.536 --> 31:57.177
How do you marry those two?

31:57.277 --> 31:58.117
Yes, so I'm the latter.

31:58.597 --> 32:12.982
I am no one truth, especially if you're an indigenous scholar, like indigenous studies scholar like myself, or if you focus on black history or Mexican-American history or any minority or marginalized history, you are not the spotlight.

32:13.182 --> 32:17.564
So that dominant narrative, you can go, but all day.

32:18.384 --> 32:23.546
And I think you have to accept that frustration that you have to meet that middle.

32:25.148 --> 32:46.822
and it's maybe the more uncomfortable version but trying to be respectful to both sides and not just painting one as the villain and one as the savior that doesn't do anything good that isn't honest i yeah so it's difficult and it's frustrating there's people in my cohort who roll their eyes at what i do and it's fine um

32:49.002 --> 32:55.926
Speaking more about that, the uncomfortableness in history, because there's facts and events that are uncomfortable.

32:56.586 --> 33:00.468
What would you tell people who are uncomfortable when they're learning these things?

33:00.528 --> 33:01.349
That's wonderful.

33:01.889 --> 33:02.549
It means you're human.

33:04.030 --> 33:04.790
It means you're human.

33:05.911 --> 33:13.055
I am so glad that you can walk through the Holocaust Museum and go, that's horrific in tears.

33:13.615 --> 33:14.896
That means you're human.

33:14.916 --> 33:15.016
Yeah.

33:18.462 --> 33:23.703
I think expecting entertainment from history, okay, go to the movies for that.

33:25.744 --> 33:29.404
If you want to feel good about history, that's just propaganda.

33:30.225 --> 33:31.025
Read a book for that.

33:31.085 --> 33:32.285
Read a fiction book for that.

33:33.105 --> 33:41.287
I just don't think history is necessarily the outlet for entertainment, for pleasure, for, you know, if you're interested in the sublime, read Lovecraft.

33:41.307 --> 33:43.227
You know what I mean?

33:44.048 --> 33:45.248
It's just not, I think...

33:46.225 --> 33:49.888
something that needs a casual viewing.

33:49.988 --> 33:53.871
I think it's a very serious, almost sacred thing.

33:53.911 --> 33:59.234
That doesn't mean that only learned people or the highest of people get to touch it or interact with it.

33:59.515 --> 34:07.821
I am a huge proponent for public history, but I think it's the open mindedness of knowing that there's a million truths to this one statement.

34:08.421 --> 34:12.084
And there's a million historians who would write that sentence completely differently.

34:12.444 --> 34:13.024
And that's beautiful.

34:13.184 --> 34:13.905
I find that beautiful.

34:15.665 --> 34:38.140
actually yeah they're like we were explaining this um and for a student that i was teeing for and she said like what do you mean when we say historians debate the date things happen and i said okay 9 11 always happened on september 11 2001 we're not debating that we're debating

34:39.232 --> 34:40.192
When did something start?

34:40.332 --> 34:41.433
When did that movement end?

34:41.513 --> 34:42.873
When did the war on terror end?

34:42.973 --> 34:44.113
Who are the players involved?

34:44.133 --> 34:45.373
That's what we're talking about.

34:46.414 --> 35:02.837
